# Design: The rebuildable manifest cache keyed by review context

## Problem
P-APPLY measured a fresh `margin review` process that rederived the
grouped manifest its own plan was composed from: `pinned_review` runs
`build_grouped_manifest` on every invocation, and the diff, the plan
validation, the resume, and the TUI finish each paid the whole tree
diff, blob reads, Markdown grouping, and per-group apply proofs again
for the same pinned comparison.

## Approach
One cache entry per pinned comparison, stored under
`$GIT_DIR/margin/manifest-cache/<key>`, where the key is the Git-hashed
canonical bytes of the review context (branch, B, C, scope, engine,
Git version) under its own domain `margin-review-cache-v1` — the review
identity's inputs minus the manifest digest, so the entry can be found
before the manifest exists. SPEC 8.2 lists rebuildable caches as allowed
disposable state; SPEC 13.3 names exactly this cache ("cache by
B/C/scope/engine"; "a cache miss may be slower but cannot change
correctness").

Verification on read, in order: strict parse (deny_unknown_fields),
context equality with the live comparison, manifest digest recomputed
from the stored groups, review identity recomputed from the context and
that digest, every change identity recomputed in one batched hash, and
one batched `cat-file --batch-check` that B, C, and every blob the
derivation read are still in the store. Structural checks refuse an
entry whose hunk indexes point outside the file, whose group lost its
patch unit, whose blob fields are not full object IDs, or whose hunk
offset+length overflow. Any refusal is a silent miss and a full
rederivation through the one authority.

## Key Trade-offs
- A cache entry is only as trustworthy as the digests it recomputes, so
  the read path costs four hash processes and one batch-check; that is
  ~20-40 ms against 160 ms (C1) / 1200 ms (C2) of derivation.
- Presentation fields (headings, excerpts) are not identity-covered; an
  accidentally corrupted presentation would be served. The identities,
  the patch bytes, the order, and the plan validation are covered, so
  approval semantics cannot move; a same-user forger is outside the
  trust boundary of SPEC 14.6 (they could rewrite .git directly).
- Entries are bounded (6 entries, 64 MiB total, 32 MiB per entry);
  eviction is by mtime with a name tie-break. A C3-scale manifest is
  simply never stored.
- The bench fixture clears the cache before every timed P-DIFF run so
  that metric keeps measuring the cold derivation its budget covers,
  while P-APPLY measures the real diff-then-review continuity.

